          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കതിര് (katirŭ) — \"an ear of ripe paddy\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, matching Tamil katir, and the same word names a ray of light. The ear of grain and the sunbeam are one shape to this language: a narrow bright thing thrown out from a stem."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Veyil, the sunshine-word, is the other half of that picture, and a Malayalam poet can put the two side by side and let the reader do the joining."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A field of കതിര് (katirŭ) is a field ready. Everything in this chapter after this point is what happens next."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കതിര് (katirŭ) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil katir and Kannada kadir (DEDR 1194). DEDR 1193 lists the same shape meaning \"ray of light\", with Tamil katir."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three."
            }
