          "title": "You'll want to know: കലപ്പ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കലപ്പ (kalappa) — \"a plough\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, and it names the tool behind a word you already carry. Karṣakan, the farmer, was named for the furrow rather than for the crop; കലപ്പ (kalappa) is what cuts the furrow."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kerala's is a light wooden one, made for ground already under water, with a single iron-shod point and a beam for a pair of cattle."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which puts the paśu back to work: the animal of the first chapter in this run and the tool of the last one are yoked together."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കലപ്പ (kalappa) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil kalappai (DEDR 1304)."
            }
          ]
        },
