        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: കൊയ്ത്ത്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കൊയ്ത്ത് (koyttŭ) — \"the harvest, the reaping\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, made from koyyuka, to cut or to reap — the harvest named for the cutting rather than for the gathering that follows it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kerala's largest festival is the harvest festival, and the flower pattern laid at the doorway for it, pūkkaḷaṁ, is the one the flower-lesson already named."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the run closes where the year does: the grain, the grass, the ripe ear, the plough that opened the ground, and the cutting that ends it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കൊയ്ത്ത് (koyttŭ) is native Dravidian, a noun from the verb \"to cut, to reap\"; DEDR 2119 lists the root with Tamil koy, Kannada koy and Telugu kōyu."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five, and the paddy year is whole: nellŭ, pullŭ, katirŭ, kalappa, koyttŭ."
            }
          ]
        },
